# More info: https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#dockerignore-file
# Ignore everything by default and re-include only needed files
**

# Re-include Go source files (but not *_test.go)
!**/*.go
**/*_test.go

# ...but not the one vendored inside the reference tenant's node_modules. The
# build context is the repository root now that the module root is, and
# `!**/*.go` reaches everywhere.
app/**

# Re-include Go module files
!go.mod
!go.sum

# And the SQL the stores embed.
#
# go:embed is a compile-time read of the filesystem, so a migration missing from
# the build context is not a runtime surprise — it is "pattern migrations/*.sql:
# no matching files found" and the build stops. That is the good version of this
# failure, and it stayed invisible for a different reason: the operator does not
# import the stores, and until 2026-09-01 the operator was the only thing with a
# Dockerfile. The first attempt to containerise the control plane hit it
# immediately.
!**/migrations/*.sql

# The panel, which package panel embeds the same way. A control plane built
# without these starts, serves the API, and returns nothing at "/" — which is
# worse than failing to build, because it looks like it worked.
!panel/**

# The one-click catalogue, which -catalog-dir points at inside the image.
#
# Re-included by name because this file ignores everything by default. Without
# it the image builds, starts, serves the API, and answers 503 on /catalog with
# "no templates are mounted" — an install that looks configured and offers
# nothing, which is the same failure the panel's re-inclusion above records.
#
# The README and the licence come with them on purpose: the image is a
# redistribution of Apache-2.0 work, and the licence has to travel with it.
!catalog/templates/**
